export const DATA_63: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-082", num: 82, title: "Đề 082", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Karl Benson and His Paranormal Research", paragraphs: ["Karl Benson is a fifty-two-year-old former high school science teacher from Bristol, England, who has spent the last eighteen years investigating reports of paranormal activity across the United Kingdom. What began as a personal curiosity following a strange experience in his own home eventually became a full-time passion that has taken him to over two hundred locations, including reportedly haunted castles, abandoned hospitals, and remote farmhouses where residents claim to have witnessed unexplained events. Although Karl does not hold a university degree in any relevant scientific field, he has read widely in psychology, physics, and folklore, and he considers himself a serious and disciplined investigator rather than an enthusiast seeking thrills.", "Karl's approach to paranormal investigation is methodical and somewhat unusual compared to what most people imagine when they think of ghost hunters. He does not arrive at a location expecting to find evidence of the supernatural. Instead, he begins every investigation by trying to identify rational explanations for whatever has been reported. He interviews residents and witnesses in detail, asking about the age and construction of the building, recent changes to the plumbing or electrical systems, nearby roads or industrial facilities, and the psychological state of those who reported the experience. In many cases — he estimates around seventy percent — he is able to identify a plausible natural cause for the reported phenomena, such as infrasound from industrial equipment, carbon monoxide leaks, or the settling of old structures in cold weather.", "When a rational explanation cannot be found, Karl sets up a range of instruments to monitor the location over several nights. These include temperature sensors, electromagnetic field detectors, audio recorders capable of capturing sounds below the threshold of human hearing, and motion-activated cameras. He keeps meticulous records of all readings and cross-references them with historical records of the location, local weather conditions, and the timing of reported incidents. He is careful to note that unusual readings on his instruments do not constitute proof of paranormal activity — they simply indicate anomalies that require further investigation.", "Over the course of his career, Karl has documented approximately thirty cases that he considers genuinely unexplained after exhausting all the rational explanations he can think of. He is cautious about what he claims these cases represent, however. He does not assert that ghosts or spirits exist; he simply says that he has encountered phenomena for which he currently has no satisfactory explanation. In one case, he recorded a consistent drop in temperature in a specific corner of a Victorian-era house every night between two and three in the morning, a pattern that persisted for three consecutive weeks and could not be linked to any identifiable heat source, air current, or building defect. He has published accounts of cases like this on his website and in a self-published book, and he regularly gives talks at local history societies and paranormal interest groups.", "Karl's work has attracted both admiration and criticism. Skeptics argue that his methods, while more rigorous than most amateur investigators, still fall far short of the standards required for scientific proof, and that his willingness to describe cases as \"unexplained\" risks encouraging belief in the supernatural among people who are already predisposed to accept it. Supporters, on the other hand, point out that Karl is doing exactly what a careful observer should do: documenting anomalies honestly rather than either dismissing them without investigation or inflating them into dramatic claims. Several local councils and heritage organizations have found his historical research genuinely useful, as his investigations often uncover forgotten details about the history of old buildings and the communities that once lived in them.", "When asked why he continues his work despite the lack of definitive conclusions, Karl gives a characteristically measured answer. He says that the most valuable thing his investigations have taught him is how much human perception is shaped by expectation, emotion, and context — and how often people experience something genuinely unusual without it being the result of anything supernatural. For Karl, the most interesting thing about paranormal reports is not the possibility that they reveal the existence of ghosts, but what they reveal about the way human beings experience and interpret the world around them."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main topic of the passage?", options: ["The history of paranormal research in the United Kingdom from the nineteenth century to the present.", "A scientific debate about whether paranormal phenomena such as ghosts and spirits can be proven to exist.", "An introduction to Karl Benson, a former teacher who investigates paranormal reports using a careful and methodical approach.", "A comparison of amateur and professional paranormal investigators and the methods they use."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"he\" in paragraph 1 refers to _______.", options: ["A resident who reported a strange experience to Karl Benson at one of his investigation sites.", "Karl Benson himself, describing his self-perception as a serious and disciplined investigator.", "A university professor who inspired Karl Benson to take up paranormal investigation.", "A skeptic who challenged Karl Benson's methods and conclusions."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, approximately what percentage of reported paranormal cases does Karl find a plausible natural explanation for?", options: ["About seventy percent.", "Approximately sixty percent.", "Around fifty percent.", "Nearly ninety percent."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following instruments does Karl use during overnight monitoring at a location?", options: ["Thermal imaging drones, voice recorders, and radiation detectors.", "Chemical analysis kits, night-vision goggles, and portable weather stations.", "High-resolution video cameras, seismographs, and ultraviolet light sensors.", "Electromagnetic field detectors, motion-activated cameras, and audio recorders for sounds below human hearing."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does Karl describe the approximately thirty cases he considers genuinely unexplained?", options: ["He asserts that they provide strong evidence for the existence of ghosts and spirits.", "He says they are phenomena for which he currently has no satisfactory explanation, but he does not claim ghosts exist.", "He claims they represent a new category of natural phenomenon that science has not yet identified.", "He argues that they require further study by professional scientists with university qualifications."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was unusual about the temperature pattern Karl recorded in a Victorian-era house?", options: ["The entire house became significantly colder whenever a member of the resident family entered the room.", "The temperature in the house dropped sharply whenever Karl's electromagnetic field detectors registered an unusual reading.", "The house was significantly colder in summer than in winter, a pattern that could not be explained by its construction.", "A specific corner of the house showed a consistent temperature drop every night between two and three in the morning for three consecutive weeks."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason why Karl's supporters value his work?", options: ["He has successfully persuaded several universities to fund further scientific investigation of his unexplained cases.", "His historical research has uncovered useful forgotten details about old buildings.", "Local councils and heritage organizations have found his research practically useful.", "He documents anomalies honestly without either dismissing them or exaggerating them."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"cross-references\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Publishes for public access in academic journals and local history newsletters.", "Compares and checks against information from other separate sources.", "Submits to independent experts for verification and peer review.", "Stores in a secure database for future access by other researchers."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"predisposed\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Already inclined or likely to believe something before examining the evidence.", "Emotionally upset and therefore unable to think clearly.", "Recently influenced by a dramatic personal experience.", "Educated enough to evaluate claims critically and independently."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's main purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To present Karl Benson as an example of a thoughtful, balanced approach to investigating unusual phenomena.", "To argue that paranormal phenomena are real and deserve more serious scientific attention.", "To warn readers about the dangers of believing in the supernatural without sufficient evidence.", "To compare Karl Benson's methods with those of professional scientists and explain why they fall short."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Modern Problem of Sleep Deprivation", paragraphs: ["Sleep is one of the most fundamental biological requirements of human life, yet it is also one of the most commonly and casually sacrificed in the name of productivity, entertainment, and the relentless demands of modern schedules. The World Health Organization has described insufficient sleep as a global epidemic affecting people in industrialized nations across all age groups, and large-scale surveys consistently report that a significant proportion of adults in developed countries sleep fewer than the seven to nine hours per night that most health authorities recommend. Understanding what happens to the body and brain when sleep is chronically curtailed — and why modern life so systematically undermines the conditions necessary for adequate rest — has become one of the most urgent areas of contemporary public health research.", "The immediate physiological effects of sleep deprivation are well documented and begin to manifest after as little as one night of reduced sleep. Reaction times slow, attention becomes fragmented, and the ability to form new memories and consolidate learning is significantly impaired. The prefrontal cortex — the region of the brain responsible for judgment, impulse control, and complex decision-making — is particularly sensitive to sleep loss, a finding that helps explain why sleep-deprived individuals not only perform cognitive tasks less accurately but also consistently underestimate how impaired they are. This phenomenon of impaired self-assessment under sleep deprivation is considered by researchers to be one of its most dangerous features, since it removes the subjective signal that would otherwise prompt a person to seek rest.", "Chronic sleep deprivation — defined broadly as consistently sleeping significantly less than one's individual optimal amount over weeks, months, or years — carries health consequences that extend far beyond tiredness and reduced cognitive performance. Epidemiological studies have linked chronic insufficient sleep to a substantially elevated risk of type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, obesity, and certain forms of cancer. The mechanisms connecting sleep loss to these outcomes are multiple: disrupted sleep impairs the regulation of the hormones leptin and ghrelin, which control appetite and satiety, promoting overeating; it elevates cortisol and inflammatory markers associated with cardiovascular damage; and it interferes with the immune system's capacity to mount effective responses to infection and cellular repair. Researchers at the University of California, Berkeley, have described chronic sleep deprivation as one of the most significant and underappreciated risk factors for premature mortality in the developed world.", "The social and occupational context in which modern sleep deprivation occurs is inseparable from understanding its causes. The invention and proliferation of electric light fundamentally altered the relationship between human sleep patterns and the natural light-dark cycle, and the subsequent development of digital screens — smartphones, tablets, and computers — has compounded this disruption by exposing the brain to blue-wavelength light at precisely the hours when the body's circadian system is preparing for sleep. The cultural valorization of long working hours and the equation of sleep with laziness — captured in phrases such as \"I'll sleep when I'm dead\" — creates social environments in which sleep deprivation is normalized, rewarded, and even admired. Shift work, which disrupts the natural circadian rhythm of approximately fifteen to twenty percent of the workforce in industrialized countries, presents particular challenges for which the human biology of sleep was simply not designed.", "Adolescents face a distinct and particularly acute version of the sleep deprivation problem. Biological changes during puberty cause a genuine delay in the circadian phase of teenagers — their bodies are programmed to fall asleep and wake up later than adults — yet most secondary school systems require students to begin classes at times that are fundamentally misaligned with adolescent biology. Studies conducted in districts in the United States that shifted high school start times to 8:30 a.m. or later found measurable improvements in academic performance, attendance, mental health outcomes, and even rates of motor vehicle accidents among teenage drivers. Despite this evidence, resistance to changing school start times remains strong, rooted in logistical concerns and the cultural assumption that early rising is a virtue rather than a biological mismatch.", "The treatment of sleep deprivation in contemporary society reveals a broader pattern of addressing symptoms rather than structural causes. The global market for sleep aids — including prescription medications, over-the-counter supplements, weighted blankets, white noise machines, and sleep-tracking wearables — is valued at tens of billions of dollars annually, reflecting enormous consumer demand for solutions to a problem that is, in most cases, better addressed by changing the conditions that cause it than by purchasing products designed to manage it. Genuine improvements in population sleep health are likely to require not only individual behavioral change but structural interventions: later school start times, reformed working hour norms, better protection for shift workers, and a cultural recalibration of the relationship between productivity and rest.", "The science of sleep is unambiguous on one point: there is no evidence that human beings can be trained to function well on significantly less sleep than their biology requires, nor is there any substitute for the complex neurological and physiological restorative processes that only occur during natural sleep. In a society that routinely treats sleep as an optional extra rather than a biological necessity, making that case loudly and repeatedly may be among the most important contributions that public health science can make."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["A comparison of sleep habits across different countries and age groups, showing which populations are most severely affected.", "A comprehensive examination of sleep deprivation — its effects on the body and brain, its social causes, and the structural changes needed to address it.", "An argument that digital technology is the single most important cause of the modern sleep deprivation epidemic.", "A review of the best medical treatments currently available for people suffering from insomnia and other sleep disorders."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is the impaired self-assessment that accompanies sleep deprivation considered especially dangerous?", options: ["It causes the prefrontal cortex to permanently reduce its capacity for complex decision-making over time.", "It causes people to attempt activities that are physically dangerous, such as driving at high speed.", "It prevents sleep-deprived individuals from recognizing that they need rest, removing the signal that would prompt them to sleep.", "It makes people more susceptible to false memories and unreliable eyewitness testimony."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following health conditions is NOT mentioned as being linked to chronic sleep deprivation in epidemiological studies?", options: ["Type 2 diabetes.", "Obesity.", "Cardiovascular disease.", "Chronic kidney disease."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what improvements were found in districts in the United States that shifted high school start times to 8:30 a.m. or later?", options: ["Reductions in student smartphone use and improvements in classroom concentration.", "Higher rates of physical activity and lower rates of adolescent obesity.", "Improvements in academic performance, attendance, mental health, and rates of motor vehicle accidents.", "Increased teacher satisfaction and lower staff absenteeism across the school year."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, approximately what percentage of the workforce in industrialized countries does shift work affect?", options: ["Between twenty-five and thirty percent.", "Approximately fifteen to twenty percent.", "Around five to ten percent.", "Over forty percent in certain industries."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a product in the global sleep aid market?", options: ["White noise machines.", "Weighted blankets.", "Virtual reality relaxation programs.", "Sleep-tracking wearable devices."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"sacrificed\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Given up or traded away, often for something considered more important.", "Studied carefully by scientists trying to understand modern health problems.", "Protected and preserved at the expense of other activities or commitments.", "Celebrated and treated as a sign of personal strength and discipline."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"sensitive\" in paragraph 2 refers to the fact that the prefrontal cortex is _______.", options: ["Responsible for processing emotional responses to frightening or stressful situations.", "Capable of detecting subtle changes in the body's chemical balance during sleep.", "Particularly vulnerable to impairment when a person does not get enough sleep.", "The most recently evolved region of the human brain and therefore the most fragile."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"inseparable\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Deeply controversial and the subject of ongoing scientific disagreement.", "Recently established through research and not yet widely recognized.", "Too complex for most people to understand without a scientific background.", "So closely connected that the two things cannot be properly understood independently."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the main obstacle to improving sleep health at a population level?", options: ["The main obstacle is cultural and structural resistance to the systemic changes — such as later school start times and reformed working hours — that evidence shows would be most effective.", "The greatest challenge is convincing individuals to purchase the sleep aid products that have been proven to work.", "The main obstacle is that no effective treatments for chronic sleep deprivation currently exist.", "The primary barrier is a lack of scientific research demonstrating the consequences of insufficient sleep."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Improving Education for Children in Remote Areas", paragraphs: ["Access to quality education remains one of the most profound and persistent inequalities dividing the world's populations. For the hundreds of millions of children living in geographically isolated communities — mountain villages, remote islands, nomadic communities in arid zones, and dispersed rural settlements far from the nearest town — the gap between the education available to them and that available to their urban counterparts is not simply a matter of school quality but often a matter of whether any schooling exists at all. Bridging this gap has become one of the central preoccupations of international development organizations, national governments, and education researchers, who have increasingly come to recognize that the solutions required are not simple extensions of urban educational models but fundamentally different approaches tailored to the realities of remote life.", "The most immediate barrier facing remote communities is the physical absence of schools and qualified teachers. In many low- and middle-income countries, rural schools are severely under-resourced: they may lack textbooks, electricity, and sanitation, be staffed by teachers with limited professional training, or serve catchment areas so large and sparsely populated that attending school requires children to walk several hours each way. [1] Absenteeism among both students and teachers is significantly higher in remote schools than in urban ones, driven by poor roads, seasonal flooding, agricultural labor demands on children's time, and the difficulty of attracting and retaining qualified educators to isolated postings that typically offer lower pay, fewer career development opportunities, and pronounced social isolation.", "Multigrade teaching — in which a single teacher instructs students of several different ages and academic levels simultaneously within the same classroom — has emerged as one of the most widely adopted adaptations to the realities of small, isolated schools. [2] When implemented well, multigrade classrooms can actually offer pedagogical advantages over single-grade instruction: older students who assist younger ones consolidate their own learning, mixed-age peer interaction enriches social development, and teachers who know their students across multiple years develop deeper and more responsive relationships with individual learners. The challenge lies in developing the curriculum materials and teacher training programs necessary to make multigrade instruction genuinely effective rather than simply a resource-constrained compromise.", "Distance learning technologies have offered a successive series of potential solutions to the challenge of remote education, each transformative in its promise and each constrained in its reach by the infrastructural limitations of the communities it was designed to serve. Radio-based education programs, pioneered in Latin America in the 1970s and 1980s, demonstrated that structured academic instruction could be delivered effectively to isolated communities without physical schools or trained teachers, and their legacy has informed the design of subsequent technology-based approaches. Satellite television brought visual instruction to communities beyond the reach of terrestrial broadcasting, and the internet has — in principle — placed the entire accumulated knowledge of human civilization within reach of anyone with a connected device. [3]", "In practice, the promise of technology-based solutions to remote education has repeatedly outpaced the conditions necessary for their realization. As of 2022, the United Nations estimated that approximately 2.9 billion people — roughly thirty-seven percent of the world's population — remained without reliable internet access, with the highest concentrations of unconnected populations in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia, precisely the regions with the largest numbers of children in remote and underserved communities. Even where connectivity exists, the devices, electricity, and digital literacy required to leverage it cannot be assumed. The COVID-19 pandemic exposed the depth of this digital divide with particular starkness: as schools closed worldwide and education shifted online, the children least able to access remote learning were typically those whose educational disadvantage was already most acute.", "Community-based solutions that engage local knowledge, language, and social structures have increasingly been recognized as indispensable complements to both physical school provision and technology-based approaches. Programs that train community members as paraprofessional educators — equipping local residents with structured teaching guides, supervised by trained teachers through periodic visits or remote communication — have shown promising results in several countries, including Bolivia, Ethiopia, and Bangladesh. These programs are grounded in the recognition that formal educational systems have historically been designed around the cultural assumptions of urban, majority populations and that genuinely inclusive education for remote communities requires not only physical access but a curriculum that acknowledges and builds on the knowledge, languages, and ways of knowing that children bring from their home environments. [4]", "The aspiration of universal quality education for children in remote areas is not, in itself, a technically impossible goal. The evidence base for what works — multigrade pedagogy, community-based teacher training, appropriate technology, mother-tongue instruction, and school feeding programs that address the interaction between hunger and learning — is substantial and growing. What remains scarce is not knowledge but political will: the sustained commitment of governments and international institutions to prioritize the educational needs of the most geographically and socially marginalized children, whose small numbers and dispersed location make them easy to overlook in the allocation of limited educational budgets."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the main focus of the passage?", options: ["A comparison of educational outcomes between children in remote rural areas and those in urban environments across different countries.", "An argument that digital technology is the only viable long-term solution to providing education in remote areas.", "A comprehensive examination of the barriers to education in remote communities and the range of approaches being used to address them.", "A criticism of international development organizations for failing to prioritize remote education in their funding programs."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as a potential pedagogical advantage of multigrade teaching?", options: ["It enables teachers to use standardized national curricula without modification or additional materials.", "Older students who help younger classmates strengthen their own understanding in the process.", "Students in multigrade classrooms consistently outperform those in single-grade classrooms on national examinations.", "It allows governments to reduce the total number of teachers required and therefore lower educational spending."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, where and when were radio-based education programs first pioneered?", options: ["In South Asia in the 1990s, following the expansion of transistor radio ownership.", "In Southeast Asia in the early 2000s, supported by UNESCO regional programs.", "In Latin America in the 1970s and 1980s.", "In sub-Saharan Africa in the 1960s, as part of post-independence literacy campaigns."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a cause of high absenteeism in remote schools?", options: ["The difficulty of attracting and retaining qualified teachers to isolated postings.", "A lack of school feeding programs that causes children to prioritize food security over attendance.", "Poor roads and seasonal flooding that make travel to school difficult.", "Agricultural labor demands on children's time during planting and harvest seasons."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what proportion of the world's population lacked reliable internet access as of 2022?", options: ["Nearly fifty percent, making remote education technology inaccessible to the majority of the world.", "Approximately twenty percent, concentrated mainly in rural parts of Asia.", "Roughly thirty-seven percent, with the highest concentrations in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia.", "Around twenty-five percent, with the largest gaps in Latin America and South Asia."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which countries are mentioned as having shown promising results with community paraprofessional educator programs?", options: ["India, Kenya, and Colombia.", "Vietnam, Ghana, and Pakistan.", "Nepal, Mozambique, and Peru.", "Bolivia, Ethiopia, and Bangladesh."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"fundamentally\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Primarily for economic reasons related to cost-effectiveness and budget constraints.", "In a way that is temporary and subject to revision as more evidence becomes available.", "In a way that is basic and deep-rooted, affecting the essential nature of something.", "Gradually and through a series of small, incremental adjustments."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"responsive\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Aware of and able to adjust appropriately to the individual needs of learners.", "Committed to maintaining consistent expectations regardless of individual circumstances.", "Formally qualified and holding recognized professional teaching credentials.", "Based on regular standardized assessment and data-driven intervention."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"complements\" in paragraph 6 refers to the role of community-based solutions as _______.", options: ["Government-endorsed alternatives that have been formally adopted as the primary strategy by international organizations.", "Replacements for physical schools and technology-based programs in communities where neither is viable.", "Low-cost compromises introduced when better-resourced solutions have proven too expensive to implement.", "Essential additions that work alongside physical school provision and technology approaches rather than substituting for them."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'In some of the most severely affected communities, surveys found that fewer than one in five students had completed a single structured learning activity during the months their schools were closed.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Growing Threats Facing Leopards: A Species at the Crossroads", paragraphs: ["The leopard ( Panthera pardus ) is, by most measures, the most ecologically versatile of the world's large cats. Capable of surviving in habitats ranging from equatorial rainforests and high-altitude mountain ranges to semi-arid savannahs and the periurban fringes of cities across Africa and Asia, it has outlasted the local extinction of virtually every other large predator across its historical range. Yet this extraordinary adaptability has not insulated the species from the converging pressures of the twenty-first century. [1] The most recent comprehensive assessment by the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) classifies the leopard as Vulnerable globally, but the nine recognized subspecies tell a markedly different story: several, including the Amur leopard of the Russian Far East, the Arabian leopard of the Arabian Peninsula, and the Sri Lankan leopard, are classified as Critically Endangered, with wild populations numbering in the dozens or low hundreds.", "Habitat loss and fragmentation constitute the most pervasive and structurally intractable of the threats the species faces. Across its range — which once extended from the Atlantic coast of West Africa through the Middle East, Central Asia, and the Indian subcontinent to the Pacific coast of the Russian Far East — the leopard has lost an estimated seventy-five percent of its historical range over the past century. The primary drivers are the conversion of natural land to agricultural use, the expansion of human settlements and infrastructure, and the extraction of timber and other natural resources that reduces the structural complexity and prey availability of forests. [2] Where habitat patches remain, they are frequently isolated by roads, farms, and built environments that leopards are reluctant or unable to cross, creating fragmented populations whose long-term genetic viability is severely compromised by reduced gene flow between isolated groups.", "Human-leopard conflict is the second major threat and, in many landscapes, the more immediately lethal one. As leopard habitat contracts and prey populations decline due to bushmeat hunting, leopards are increasingly compelled to enter agricultural and pastoral areas in search of food, preying on livestock, dogs, and — in a small number of documented cases — people. Retaliatory killing by farmers and herders who have lost animals to leopard predation is widespread across sub-Saharan Africa, the Indian subcontinent, and parts of Southeast Asia, and it accounts for a significant proportion of annual leopard mortality. In India, where human-leopard encounters occur with particular frequency in areas where forest edges abut densely populated agricultural land, several hundred leopards are killed each year in retaliatory attacks or through deliberate poisoning of carcasses left as bait. [3]", "The illegal wildlife trade constitutes a third, partially overlapping threat. Leopard skins, bones, claws, and teeth are traded across international networks supplying demand in East Asia, where they are used as substitutes for tiger parts in traditional medicine and decorative products. The trade is sustained in part by the decimation of tiger populations, which has elevated the market value of leopard products as a lower-cost alternative. Seizure data compiled by the wildlife monitoring organization TRAFFIC suggests that hundreds of leopards are killed for the illegal trade annually across Africa and Asia, though the covert nature of the networks involved makes accurate quantification difficult. Poaching pressure is not evenly distributed: subspecies with small, geographically concentrated populations — such as the Amur leopard — are particularly vulnerable to the disproportionate impact that even modest levels of offtake can have on a population already at the margins of viability.", "Conservation responses to these threats have been varied in their approaches and uneven in their effectiveness. Protected area networks remain the foundation of most national leopard conservation strategies, but the species' wide-ranging behavior means that individual leopards routinely move across the boundaries of protected areas into unprotected and often hostile landscapes. [4] Transboundary conservation initiatives — formal agreements between neighboring countries to coordinate management of shared leopard populations — have shown promising results in Central Asia, where agreements between countries including Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, and Afghanistan have supported the recovery of snow leopard populations in the Pamir and Tien Shan mountain ranges. Community-based conservation programs that provide financial compensation to herders for livestock losses attributable to leopards have demonstrated measurable reductions in retaliatory killing in several pilot areas in India and Kenya.", "Genetic research has emerged as an increasingly valuable tool in leopard conservation, enabling scientists to map population connectivity, identify isolated subpopulations at elevated risk of inbreeding depression, and design wildlife corridor interventions with precision that field surveys alone cannot achieve. DNA extracted from non-invasive samples — feces, hair, saliva collected from scratch marks on trees — allows researchers to monitor elusive populations without the disturbance costs associated with trapping and telemetry. In the Aravalli mountain range of northwestern India, genetic analysis has revealed that a population previously believed to be isolated from adjacent groups maintains limited but critical genetic connectivity through a narrow corridor of forest fragments, informing a targeted land acquisition campaign by the state forest department to secure and widen that corridor.", "Whether the leopard's legendary adaptability will prove sufficient to navigate the particular constellation of pressures it now faces is a question that conservation science cannot answer with confidence. What it can say is that the species' continued survival across its remaining range will depend not on its biology alone but on the decisions made by governments, communities, and individuals in the landscapes where leopards and people must find ways to share the same diminishing space."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the central focus of the passage?", options: ["A celebration of the leopard's extraordinary ecological adaptability and its proven capacity to survive human encroachment.", "A proposal for a single unified global conservation strategy to replace the current fragmented national approaches.", "An argument that the leopard is already functionally extinct across most of its historical range and that conservation efforts have come too late.", "A comprehensive examination of the threats facing leopards globally, the conservation responses underway, and the uncertainties that remain about the species' future."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, approximately what proportion of the leopard's historical range has been lost over the past century?", options: ["Approximately sixty percent.", "An estimated seventy-five percent.", "Around fifty percent.", "Nearly ninety percent, leaving the species confined to a handful of protected areas."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following leopard body parts is NOT mentioned as being traded in the illegal wildlife trade?", options: ["Claws.", "Skins.", "Eyes.", "Bones."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what drives the market demand for leopard parts in the illegal wildlife trade?", options: ["A growing interest in leopard-print fashion and luxury goods among wealthy consumers in Europe and North America.", "A tradition of wearing leopard skins as status symbols among political and religious leaders in several African countries.", "Demand in East Asia for substitutes for tiger parts in traditional medicine and decorative products, sustained by the decimation of tiger populations.", "The use of leopard bones in industrial pharmaceutical manufacturing processes that have no legal substitute."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, in which mountain ranges have transboundary conservation agreements shown promising results for snow leopard recovery?", options: ["The Altai mountains, through a bilateral agreement between Russia and Mongolia.", "The Caucasus mountains, through a joint initiative between Russia, Georgia, and Azerbaijan.", "The Pamir and Tien Shan mountain ranges, through agreements involving Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, and Afghanistan.", "The Himalayas and the Hindu Kush, supported by agreements between Nepal, Pakistan, and India."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"ecologically versatile\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Resistant to disease and able to recover quickly from population decline.", "Highly specialized in its hunting techniques and prey selection.", "Exceptionally fast and difficult to track or observe in the wild.", "Able to adapt and survive across a wide variety of different environments."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"overlapping\" in paragraph 4 refers to the fact that the illegal wildlife trade _______.", options: ["Is carried out by the same individuals who are responsible for retaliatory killings of leopards.", "Occurs exclusively in the same geographic regions where habitat loss and human-leopard conflict are most severe.", "Shares some causal factors and geographic areas with the other threats already discussed in the passage.", "Has completely replaced habitat loss as the primary cause of leopard population decline in Asia."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"disproportionate\" in paragraph 4 refers to the fact that even modest levels of poaching can have _______.", options: ["An economic impact on local communities that exceeds the direct biological damage to the leopard population.", "A surprisingly limited impact on leopard populations that are already well-protected by conservation programs.", "An impact on subspecies like the Amur leopard that is far larger than the actual number of animals killed would suggest.", "A more severe effect on females than on males, disrupting breeding and reducing cub survival rates."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's view of the leopard's future survival prospects?", options: ["The author implies that the leopard's survival will ultimately depend not on the animal's own capabilities but on human choices — about land use, conservation investment, and coexistence.", "The author argues that genetic research is the single most important factor determining whether leopards can recover their historical populations.", "The author is confident that the leopard's adaptability will ensure its survival regardless of human actions.", "The author believes the species is already beyond saving in most of its range and that conservation efforts should focus only on the most viable populations."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This corridor finding has since been replicated in similar genetic assessments conducted in leopard populations in Ethiopia and across the Western Ghats mountain range in India, suggesting that connectivity-based conservation planning may offer a broadly applicable framework for fragmented leopard populations worldwide.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-122", num: 122, title: "Đề 122", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Never Lose Your Keys Again: The Rise of Bluetooth Trackers", paragraphs: ["Losing a wallet, a set of keys, or a suitcase at the airport is a frustrating experience that almost everyone has faced at least once. In the past, a lost item often meant going without it permanently or spending hours retracing one's steps. Today, however, small electronic tracking devices have changed how people manage this common problem. One of the most popular of these devices is a small, coin-sized tag that can be attached to almost any personal belonging. Once attached, the tag connects wirelessly to a smartphone app, allowing the owner to see the item's last known location on a map within seconds.", "These tracking tags do not rely on traditional GPS satellites, which would drain their tiny batteries within days. Instead, the device leverages a clever network made up of millions of other smartphones owned by strangers. When a lost tag comes within a short wireless range of any compatible phone, that phone quietly and anonymously relays the tag's location to its owner, without the stranger ever knowing or being involved. This crowd-sourced approach means that a tag dropped in a crowded city is often located within minutes, while one lost in a remote area might take longer, since fewer phones pass nearby to pick up its signal.", "Despite their convenience, these tracking tags have raised serious privacy concerns. Shortly after release, reports emerged of people finding tags hidden in their bags or attached to their cars without their knowledge. Some individuals had exploited the technology to secretly monitor partners, ex-partners, or strangers, raising alarm among privacy advocates and law enforcement agencies. Unlike a phone, which a person actively carries and controls, a coin-sized tag can be slipped into a coat pocket or taped under a vehicle bumper without the owner ever suspecting anything unusual. This unintended use of a helpful gadget for tracking people rather than possessions quickly became a topic of public debate.", "In response to mounting criticism, the manufacturer introduced several safety features designed to prevent misuse. Modern smartphones now automatically warn users if an unfamiliar tag appears to be travelling with them for an extended period, even when the tag's owner is a stranger. In addition, after a certain number of hours away from its registered owner, a tag begins to emit an audible sound, making it harder to hide; engineers deliberately built in a short delay before the sound activates. This was intended to give honest users, such as someone who simply left a tagged backpack with a friend, enough time to retrieve their belongings before being mistakenly flagged as a stalker. The updates did not eliminate every risk, but they noticeably reduced reported misuse and reassured many cautious customers.", "Beyond concerns about misuse, the vast majority of buyers use these tags exactly as intended: to keep track of everyday belongings. Travellers frequently slip a tag inside a suitcase before a flight, allowing them to confirm that their luggage has actually been loaded onto the correct plane and to locate it quickly if an airline misroutes it. Parents attach tags to their children's backpacks for peace of mind on school trips, while forgetful commuters clip one to a keyring or wallet. Pet owners have even started attaching smaller versions to collars, though manufacturers warn that the devices were never designed to replace dedicated pet-tracking collars with longer battery life and more frequent location updates.", "The popularity of these small trackers reflects a broader shift toward smart, connected objects that quietly support daily life without demanding much attention. As competitors release their own versions, prices are likely to fall, and battery life and accuracy should continue to improve. Whether this trend ultimately makes society more efficient or simply introduces new privacy headaches will depend largely on how manufacturers balance convenience with responsible safeguards in the years ahead."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Small Bluetooth tracking tags were created primarily to help law enforcement monitor suspected criminals safely.", "Small Bluetooth tracking tags have completely replaced GPS systems for tracking valuable personal belongings.", "Small Bluetooth tracking tags offer convenience but also raise privacy concerns that manufacturers have tried to address.", "Small Bluetooth tracking tags are mainly used by travelers to prevent airlines from losing their luggage."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does a lost tracking tag's location get communicated to its owner?", options: ["Local Wi-Fi routers detect the tag's signal and broadcast its location to the owner's phone.", "Nearby strangers' smartphones detect the tag and quietly forward its location without the strangers' involvement.", "The tag connects directly to satellite systems and sends its coordinates straight to the owner's phone.", "The tag stores location data internally until the owner physically retrieves and downloads the information."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did the tracking tags raise privacy concerns?", options: ["Some people used the tags to copy the personal contacts stored on other phones.", "Some people used the tags to disable the location services on other people's devices.", "Some people used the tags to access private information stored on other people's smartphones.", "Some people used the tags to secretly track other individuals rather than personal belongings."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did engineers build a delay before the tag's alert sound activates?", options: ["To give thieves enough time to disable the alert sound before it could attract attention.", "To give honest users enough time to retrieve a tagged item before being mistakenly suspected of stalking.", "To give airline staff enough time to scan the tag before loading luggage onto the plane.", "To give smartphone manufacturers enough time to update their tracking software before release."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a common use of tracking tags?", options: ["Attaching the tag to a child's backpack for added peace of mind.", "Clipping the tag onto a keyring or wallet to avoid misplacing it.", "Confirming that checked luggage was loaded onto the correct flight.", "Monitoring a vehicle's speed and location for insurance discount programs."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"leverages\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["apologizes for", "interferes with", "makes use of", "competes against"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"exploited\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["donated", "ignored", "repaired", "misused"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["the manufacturer's decision to release a cheaper version of the tag", "the process of registering a new tag to a smartphone account", "the moment a stranger's smartphone detects a nearby tag", "the short delay engineers added before the tag's sound begins"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the manufacturer's response to privacy concerns?", options: ["The updates were only released after government regulators forced the manufacturer to act immediately.", "The updates were ignored by most customers, who continued to distrust the tags' safety features.", "The updates reduced misuse but did not completely solve every safety problem the tags created.", "The updates eliminated all reported incidents of the tags being used to track people secretly."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To convince readers that tracking tags are too dangerous to be sold to ordinary consumers.", "To criticize smartphone manufacturers for failing to protect users from technological dangers entirely.", "To explain how tracking tags work and to present both their benefits and their risks fairly.", "To advertise a specific brand of tracking tag as superior to all of its competitors."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Social Lives of African Lions", paragraphs: ["Among the world's big cats, the African lion stands out for one striking reason: it is the only species that lives in large, cooperative social groups rather than alone. While tigers, leopards, and most other wild cats are solitary hunters that rarely tolerate company, lions form tight-knit groups known as prides, which can include more than a dozen individuals. This unusual social structure has fascinated biologists for decades, since it raises fundamental questions about why cooperation evolved in one big cat species but not in its close relatives. Understanding pride life offers valuable insight into the broader evolutionary trade-offs between solitary survival and group living.", "A typical pride consists predominantly of related adult females, their cubs, and a smaller number of adult males who defend the group's territory. The females, who usually remain with the same pride for life, do most of the hunting, often working together to bring down prey far larger than any single lion could overpower alone. This cooperative strategy allows prides to target animals such as buffalo and giraffe, which would be far too dangerous or simply impossible for a lone hunter to tackle successfully. Shared hunting also means shared risk, since coordinated females can retreat or regroup quickly if an ambush goes wrong, something solitary cats cannot do.", "Adult males, in contrast, rarely participate directly in hunts, focusing instead on protecting the pride from rival males and from other predators such as hyenas. Males typically form coalitions of two to four individuals, often brothers, who together stand a far better chance of holding a territory than a male attempting to rule alone. When a new coalition successfully overtakes an existing pride, their first act is often to drive out or kill any cubs fathered by the previous males, a behavior that ensures the new males' own offspring will not have to compete for limited resources. This harsh reality of pride takeovers illustrates how intense the competition for reproductive success can be among male lions.", "Lions communicate using a range of signals, the most famous being the roar, which can be heard from as far as eight kilometres away under the right conditions. A roar that remains clearly audible at such distance serves several purposes: it warns rival prides to stay away, helps separated members of the same pride locate one another, and signals a male's presence and strength to potential challengers. Beyond roaring, lions also rely heavily on scent marking, rubbing their faces against trees or spraying urine along territory boundaries to leave chemical messages that persist long after the animal has moved on. Body language, including specific tail and ear positions, communicates mood and intention during encounters with other lions.", "Despite their cultural status as a symbol of strength and royalty, African lions face serious threats to their survival in the wild. Habitat loss, driven by expanding agriculture and human settlement, has fragmented many of the open grasslands that prides depend on for hunting. As human populations grow closer to lion territories, conflict frequently follows, particularly when lions prey on livestock and farmers respond by killing the animals responsible. Poaching for body parts used in traditional medicine and trophy hunting add further pressure, and some researchers estimate that lion populations across Africa have declined by more than forty percent over the past few decades.", "Conservation organizations have responded with a combination of strategies aimed at protecting remaining lion populations. Establishing and properly funding protected reserves remains one of the most effective approaches, since lions inside well-managed parks generally fare far better than those living near unprotected farmland. Some programs also compensate farmers for livestock losses, reducing the financial incentive to kill lions in retaliation, while community-based initiatives train local residents to track and monitor prides, turning former hunters into paid conservation partners. Researchers continue to study pride dynamics and migration patterns, hoping that a deeper understanding of lion behavior will lead to more effective long-term protection strategies.", "Ultimately, the future of the African lion will depend on whether conservation efforts can keep pace with the pressures of habitat loss and human expansion. The species' complex social structure, which once seemed like a guaranteed evolutionary advantage, now means that the loss of even a few individuals, particularly dominant males, can destabilize an entire pride. Protecting lions, therefore, requires more than simply preserving land; it requires sustaining the delicate social bonds that make pride life possible in the first place."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["African lions exhibit a unique social structure among big cats, with distinct roles for males and females that face growing conservation threats.", "African lions communicate exclusively through roaring, which distinguishes them from every other species of wild cat in Africa.", "African lions have abandoned their traditional pride structure in recent decades due to shrinking habitats and human expansion.", "African lions are the most dangerous big cats in Africa because they hunt in coordinated groups that overpower nearly any prey."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are lionesses able to hunt animals such as buffalo and giraffe?", options: ["They rely on male lions to weaken large prey before lionesses finish the hunt together.", "They use scent marking to confuse large prey before launching a coordinated ambush attack.", "They coordinate their efforts as a group, allowing them to overpower prey too large for a single lion to manage.", "They are physically larger and stronger than male lions, making solo hunts of large prey possible."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what advantage do male lions gain by forming coalitions?", options: ["Coalitions allow male lions to communicate with rival prides without the risk of physical confrontation.", "Coalitions allow male lions to avoid all conflict with rival males seeking to take over a pride.", "Coalitions of brothers can hold a territory far more successfully than a single male trying to rule alone.", "Coalitions enable male lions to participate more actively in the daily hunting activities of the pride."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one function of a lion's roar?", options: ["It permanently marks a territory boundary in a way that lasts longer than any scent marking.", "It frightens away natural predators such as hyenas without requiring any physical confrontation.", "It signals to lionesses exactly when and where a hunt is about to begin each evening.", "It helps separated pride members find each other and warns rival prides to keep their distance."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a threat to African lion populations?", options: ["Habitat loss caused by expanding agriculture and human settlement.", "Conflict with farmers after lions prey on domestic livestock.", "Poaching connected to traditional medicine and trophy hunting.", "Disease outbreaks spreading rapidly between neighboring prides."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"predominantly\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["mainly", "rarely", "temporarily", "accidentally"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"audible\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["able to be seen", "able to be predicted", "able to be measured", "able to be heard"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the female lions who continue to hunt for the entire pride", "the new coalition of males that has just overtaken the pride", "the rival hyenas that compete with lions for the same prey", "the cubs fathered by the previous males before the takeover"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the impact of losing dominant males from a pride?", options: ["It can seriously disrupt the pride's social stability, since the complex structure depends on consistent leadership.", "It causes neighboring prides to immediately merge together to compensate for the missing males.", "It usually leads surviving females to abandon the pride entirely and live as solitary hunters.", "It has little overall effect, since female lions are fully capable of replacing male leadership roles."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward the future of African lions, as expressed in the passage?", options: ["Cautiously concerned, recognizing real threats while acknowledging that conservation strategies may offer a path forward.", "Entirely optimistic, suggesting that current conservation programs have already solved most major threats to lions.", "Completely indifferent, presenting facts about lions without expressing any concern for their long-term survival.", "Strongly pessimistic, arguing that no realistic conservation effort can prevent the extinction of wild lion prides."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Evolutionary Partnership Between Humans and Dogs", paragraphs: ["Long before agriculture, writing, or permanent settlements existed, an unusual alliance had already begun to take shape between humans and a wild predator. Genetic evidence suggests that dogs were domesticated from wolves at least fifteen thousand years ago, making them the oldest domesticated species on the planet by a considerable margin. Unlike cattle, sheep, or chickens, which were domesticated primarily as food sources, the earliest relationship between humans and proto-dogs appears to have been built on something closer to mutual usefulness. [1] Some researchers argue that wolves essentially domesticated themselves by scavenging near human camps, with the bolder, less fearful individuals gradually tolerating proximity to people and eventually becoming dependent on this new ecological niche .", "This self-domestication theory has gained considerable traction among evolutionary biologists, partly because it resolves a puzzle that troubled earlier explanations. [2] If humans had deliberately captured and tamed wild wolf pups, as was long assumed, the process would have required immense effort and patience across many generations, with little obvious benefit during the earliest stages. Self-domestication, by contrast, requires no deliberate human intervention at all; it simply rewards wolves that exhibit reduced flight response and increased tolerance for human presence with reliable access to food scraps. [3] Over many generations, natural selection acting on this single behavioral trait could plausibly produce animals that looked and behaved increasingly differently from their wild ancestors, even without humans ever choosing which individuals to breed.", "One of the most striking pieces of supporting evidence comes from a decades-long experiment involving foxes rather than wolves. Researchers selectively bred foxes based purely on their tameness toward humans, with no attention paid to any physical characteristic. [4] Remarkably, after only a few dozen generations, the tame foxes began to display floppy ears, curled tails, and patches of white fur, traits that closely resemble those seen in domestic dogs but that were never directly selected for. This outcome implies that selecting for tameness alone can trigger a cascade of unintended physical changes, likely because the genes governing fear and stress responses are linked to genes controlling pigmentation and skeletal development during early growth.", "As the partnership between humans and dogs deepened over thousands of years, dogs began to take on increasingly specialized roles within human societies. Early dogs likely assisted with hunting, using their superior sense of smell to track prey that would have otherwise eluded human hunters, while also serving as an early warning system against approaching predators or rival groups. As communities transitioned to agriculture, dogs took on herding duties, helping to manage livestock across open grazing land in ways that would have been far more labor-intensive for humans alone. Selective breeding, once humans began intentionally choosing which dogs to mate, accelerated dramatically during the last few centuries, producing the staggering diversity of shapes, sizes, and temperaments seen among modern breeds.", "This intentional breeding, while responsible for useful working traits such as herding instinct or scent-tracking ability, has also generated significant unintended consequences. Many modern breeds, having been selected primarily for appearance or for the exaggeration of particular physical features, now suffer from chronic health problems linked directly to their genetic makeup. Breeds developed for unusually short skulls frequently experience breathing difficulties, while breeds prized for their large size often face shortened lifespans and joint disorders. Veterinary geneticists increasingly argue that the very traits humans value most in certain breeds are inseparable from the health burdens those breeds disproportionately carry, creating a genuine ethical tension between aesthetic preference and animal welfare.", "The cognitive abilities dogs have developed alongside humans are equally remarkable, particularly when compared to those of wolves raised in similar environments. Dogs demonstrate an unusual sensitivity to human gestures and gaze, readily following a pointed finger toward hidden food, a task that even wolves raised from puppyhood by humans typically fail to master. This sensitivity to human communication likely reflects thousands of years of selection pressure favoring dogs that could anticipate and respond to human intentions, since such animals would have been more useful companions and would have received preferential care in return. Some researchers propose that dogs have effectively evolved a specialized form of social intelligence calibrated specifically toward interpreting human behavior, rather than general intelligence applicable to any species.", "Today, the relationship continues to evolve in directions early humans could scarcely have imagined, from dogs trained to detect medical conditions through scent to robotic companions designed to mimic canine loyalty. Yet the underlying dynamic, mutual dependence shaped gradually through thousands of years of close contact, remains essentially unchanged from its ancient origins. Understanding how this partnership first formed continues to offer insight not only into dog behavior but into the broader question of how cooperation between species can emerge gradually, without any single party designing the outcome in advance."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Dogs likely self-domesticated from wolves and have since developed specialized traits and cognition through their long partnership with humans.", "Dogs are physically and cognitively identical to wolves, with selective breeding having little real effect on their behavior or appearance.", "Dogs were domesticated by humans deliberately capturing wolf pups, a process that explains all modern breed diversity today.", "Dogs were domesticated primarily as a food source, similar to cattle and sheep, before later taking on working roles."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does the self-domestication theory resolve a problem with earlier explanations of dog origins?", options: ["It suggests that humans selectively bred wolves for appearance long before any working roles existed.", "It explains why modern dog breeds suffer from health problems linked to specific physical traits.", "It avoids the need for deliberate human effort across generations with little early benefit to people.", "It accounts for why dogs can interpret human gestures better than wolves raised by humans."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did researchers discover from the fox-breeding experiment?", options: ["Foxes bred for tameness retained the same physical appearance as their wild, untamed ancestors.", "Foxes bred for tameness developed physical traits resembling dogs despite no direct selection for appearance.", "Foxes bred for tameness lost their ability to survive in environments without close human contact.", "Foxes bred for tameness eventually became less responsive to human gestures than untrained wolves."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one consequence of intentional selective breeding in modern dog breeds?", options: ["Breeds developed for short skulls have lost most of their ancestral hunting and herding instincts.", "Breeds developed for short skulls have become less responsive to human commands over time.", "Breeds developed for short skulls have shown a notable reduction in joint and bone disorders.", "Breeds developed for short skulls often experience breathing difficulties linked to their genetic makeup."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a traditional or historical role of dogs?", options: ["Pulling sleds across long distances in cold northern climates.", "Serving as an early warning system against approaching predators.", "Tracking prey using a superior sense of smell during hunts.", "Helping manage livestock across open grazing land during herding."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the link between tameness and physical traits in the fox experiment?", options: ["The foxes' physical changes occurred entirely by chance and had no connection to the breeding process at all.", "Researchers intentionally selected for floppy ears and curled tails alongside tameness in every generation.", "Tameness in foxes was caused directly by changes to their fur color rather than any behavioral selection.", "Genes related to fear and stress responses may be biologically linked to genes affecting pigmentation and bone development."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"niche\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a specific ecological role or position", "a formal scientific classification", "a small decorative object", "a sudden environmental disaster"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"cascade\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a single isolated event", "a chain or series of consequences", "a carefully planned experiment", "a sudden halt or stoppage"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["the health burdens carried disproportionately by certain dog breeds", "the working traits such as herding instinct or scent-tracking", "the veterinary geneticists studying breed-related health issues", "the aesthetic preferences of dog owners and breeders"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Earlier theories, by contrast, assumed that humans actively sought out and tamed individual wolf pups from a young age.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Reimagining Architecture in an Era of Energy Constraint", paragraphs: ["For much of the twentieth century, architectural design in much of the industrialized world operated on an implicit assumption: energy was abundant, cheap, and largely irrelevant to how buildings should be shaped. [1] Glass towers sealed against the outside air, dependent entirely on mechanical heating and cooling, came to symbolize architectural modernity, their climate control made possible only by an uninterrupted and inexpensive supply of electricity. [2] This assumption, largely unquestioned for decades, has come under sustained pressure as the economic and environmental costs of energy consumption have become impossible to ignore, prompting a fundamental reconsideration of how structures ought to be conceived from their earliest design stages. [3]", "Contemporary architects increasingly distinguish between two complementary, though conceptually distinct, strategies for reducing a building's environmental footprint. The first, often termed passive design, seeks to minimize energy demand before any mechanical system is even installed, by exploiting the building's form, orientation, and materials to regulate temperature and light naturally. [4] The second strategy, active design, involves integrating renewable energy generation directly into the building itself, most commonly through photovoltaic panels or small-scale wind turbines, so that whatever energy the structure does require can be sourced on-site rather than drawn from a fossil-fuel-dependent grid. The most ambitious contemporary projects do not treat these approaches as alternatives but pursue them simultaneously, on the premise that a building should first need as little energy as possible and only then generate what remains necessary.", "Passive strategies, while seemingly modest compared to the visual drama of rooftop turbines, arguably deliver the more durable returns, precisely because they do not depend on mechanical components that degrade, require maintenance, or eventually need replacement. Thoughtful orientation toward the sun, for instance, can dramatically reduce a building's reliance on artificial heating during colder months while simultaneously limiting unwanted heat gain in summer, provided that shading elements are calibrated to block high summer sun while admitting lower winter sun. Thermal mass, achieved through dense materials such as concrete or rammed earth, absorbs heat during the day and releases it slowly at night, smoothing out temperature fluctuations without consuming any electricity whatsoever. Natural ventilation strategies, including carefully positioned openings that exploit pressure differences to draw air through a building, can in many climates eliminate the need for mechanical cooling almost entirely, though such strategies are inevitably constrained by local climate and cannot be applied uniformly across all regions.", "Active renewable systems, by contrast, address the portion of energy demand that passive design alone cannot eliminate, and their declining cost over the past decade has made them increasingly viable even for modest residential projects rather than only prestige commercial developments. Photovoltaic technology has become sufficiently unobtrusive that solar cells can now be integrated into roofing tiles, facade panels, and even window glazing, allowing energy generation to occur without the visually disruptive arrays that once defined early solar installations. Small-scale wind turbines, though generally less efficient than passive measures in urban settings due to turbulent and unpredictable airflow between buildings, can nonetheless make a meaningful contribution in more exposed rural or coastal locations where wind patterns are more consistent and predictable.", "Skeptics of this broader movement toward energy-conscious design have raised a number of substantive objections that proponents have not always adequately addressed. Some argue that the additional upfront costs associated with high-performance materials and renewable infrastructure may not be recoverable within a reasonable timeframe, particularly for budget-constrained public projects such as schools or affordable housing, where initial capital expenditure is frequently prioritized over long-term operational savings. Others contend that an excessive focus on quantifiable energy metrics risks producing buildings that satisfy narrow technical benchmarks while neglecting less measurable but equally important qualities, such as occupant wellbeing, spatial generosity, and aesthetic coherence with surrounding neighborhoods. These critiques do not necessarily undermine the broader goal of reducing energy consumption, but they do suggest that energy efficiency cannot reasonably serve as the sole criterion against which architectural success is measured.", "Despite these reservations, a growing body of evidence from completed projects suggests that energy-conscious design need not come at the expense of either affordability or architectural ambition. Several recently completed net-zero schools and housing developments have demonstrated that careful integration of passive and active strategies from the earliest design stages, rather than as costly retrofits applied after the fact, can substantially narrow the cost gap that critics frequently cite. This timing distinction appears to matter considerably: strategies woven into a building's fundamental form and orientation from the outset tend to be markedly more cost-effective than equivalent measures bolted onto an already-completed structure, since retrofitting often requires undoing existing work before improvements can even begin.", "What ultimately emerges from this ongoing debate is not a simple verdict that energy-conscious architecture is unambiguously superior or inferior to conventional design, but rather a more nuanced recognition that the timing, integration, and balance of strategies matter as much as the strategies themselves. A passive design feature poorly suited to its climate may achieve little, just as a renewable energy system retrofitted as an afterthought may fail to recoup its cost. The future of architecture, it seems, will be defined less by any single technology than by the discipline required to think holistically about energy from the very first sketch."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Renewable energy systems alone have made passive design strategies largely unnecessary in contemporary architecture.", "Energy-conscious architecture combines passive and active strategies, and their success depends heavily on early integration and balance.", "Architects have unanimously agreed that energy efficiency should be the sole criterion for judging building design.", "Passive design strategies are universally effective across all climates and therefore require no active energy systems."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why might passive design strategies offer more durable benefits than active renewable systems?", options: ["They were developed earlier and have therefore been tested over a much longer period of time.", "They do not rely on mechanical components that degrade and eventually require maintenance or replacement.", "They generate more electricity overall than photovoltaic panels or small-scale wind turbines combined.", "They are significantly cheaper to install than any form of renewable energy technology available today."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are small-scale wind turbines generally less efficient in urban settings?", options: ["Turbulent and unpredictable airflow between buildings reduces their efficiency compared to passive measures.", "Urban areas receive insufficient sunlight to power the mechanical components of wind turbines effectively.", "Local governments in urban areas have not yet approved funding for wind turbine infrastructure projects.", "Urban building codes typically prohibit the installation of wind turbines on residential structures."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what factor most influences whether energy-conscious strategies remain cost-effective?", options: ["Whether the building is intended for residential use rather than commercial or institutional purposes.", "Whether the project receives government subsidies specifically designated for renewable energy infrastructure.", "Whether the strategies rely primarily on photovoltaic technology rather than wind-based energy generation.", "Whether the strategies are integrated from the earliest design stages rather than added as later retrofits."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The passage states that some critics argue upfront costs for energy-conscious design may not be recoverable quickly. Which of the following is also true in the passage but does NOT directly address this cost concern?", options: ["Net-zero schools and housing developments have narrowed the cost gap that critics frequently cite.", "Some critics worry that energy metrics alone neglect occupant wellbeing and aesthetic coherence.", "Photovoltaic cells can now be integrated into roofing tiles and facade panels without visual disruption.", "Retrofitting existing structures often requires undoing prior work before improvements can begin."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following statements, while true according to the passage, does NOT directly explain why timing matters for cost-effectiveness?", options: ["Strategies integrated from a building's earliest form tend to be more cost-effective than later additions.", "Retrofitting often requires undoing existing work before any improvement can actually begin.", "Net-zero projects have shown that early integration can substantially narrow the cost gap critics cite.", "Thermal mass made from dense materials absorbs heat during the day and releases it slowly at night."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"unobtrusive\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["resistant to damage from weather", "highly efficient at generating power", "extremely expensive to manufacture", "not noticeable or visually disruptive"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"holistically\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["in a manner dependent entirely on government regulation", "in a manner that ignores established architectural traditions", "in a manner focused exclusively on financial cost", "in a manner considering the whole system rather than isolated parts"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["the schools and affordable housing projects with limited budgets", "the photovoltaic panels integrated into roofing tiles and facades", "the renewable energy systems installed in modest residential projects", "the objections and critiques raised by skeptics of energy-conscious design"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Few architects at the time paused to consider whether this dependence might eventually become a liability rather than a convenience.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
];
